   "about": "Continues a comprehensive survey of Japanese trade, industry, transport, communications, and commercial policy through mid-1930.",
   "summary": "The report supplies extensive statistics on wheat flour, preserved foods, cement, iron and steel, machinery, woollens, petroleum, chemicals, automobiles, motorcycles, cinema films, and radio apparatus. It emphasizes expanding Japanese domestic production, import substitution, industrial overcapacity, depressed markets, and British efforts to retain export business. Later sections examine roads, railways, merchant shipping, shipbuilding, commercial aviation, wireless services, treaties, tariffs, export-promotion legislation, and the official campaign to favor Japanese goods. It closes with notes on the 1929 World Engineering Congress and forthcoming international meetings in Tokyo.",
