  {
   "title": "Annex I: Report on the Trade of Formosa for 1929 and Conditions to June, 1930",
   "doc_type": "consular economic report",
   "start_page": 81,
   "end_page": 89,
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1930-06",
   "from": "A. R. Ovens, Acting British Consul, Tamsui",
   "to": "",
   "about": "Reports on Taiwan's trade, public finances, agriculture, industries, transportation, and commercial conditions in 1929 and early 1930.",
   "summary": "Ovens provides statistical summaries of Taiwan's population, imports, exports, principal trading partners, railway system, travel conditions, and government budget. He describes a trade surplus driven chiefly by exports to Japan, while warning of falling prices, weak purchasing power, accumulating stocks, and the island's dependence on agriculture. Detailed sections cover sugar, rice, tea, camphor, imitation Panama hats, alcohol, tinned pineapples, mineral ores, coal, cement, fertilizers, petroleum products, and motor vehicles. The report also notes government subsidies, irrigation and infrastructure works, import restrictions, and attempts to improve or diversify production and overseas markets.",
